WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:01.512
There's a documentary that has just

2
00:00:01.512 --> 00:00:02.515
filled me with delight.

3
00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:05.126
It's a Hulu documentary, many of you may know

4
00:00:05.126 --> 00:00:07.276
what I'm talking about, that's focused on

5
00:00:07.276 --> 00:00:10.288
The Dana Carvey Show, and a show that you helped,

6
00:00:10.288 --> 00:00:11.648
you really got your start.

7
00:00:11.648 --> 00:00:13.235
Yeah, I've done some work before that,

8
00:00:13.235 --> 00:00:14.917
but that was really, I got my break.

9
00:00:14.917 --> 00:00:17.989
Dana Carvey and Robert Smigel, of course, your old friend--

10
00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:20.304
Yes, Robert Smigel, a great comedy writer.

11
00:00:20.304 --> 00:00:21.800
(crowd cheers)

12
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.047
(mumbles) and Steve Carell.

13
00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:25.597
Steve Carell was in the cast.

14
00:00:25.597 --> 00:00:29.046
And I remembered this very well because I am friends

15
00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:31.198
with Dana, I remember the launch of this show.

16
00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:32.716
And the show didn't last long.

17
00:00:32.716 --> 00:00:34.398
No, no, we only shot eight of them.

18
00:00:34.398 --> 00:00:36.939
Only seven of them made it to air, yeah.

19
00:00:36.939 --> 00:00:38.667
And what's interesting about the documentary,

20
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.198
it's so fascinating to me because so many people

21
00:00:40.198 --> 00:00:42.027
are interested in the behind the scenes of comedy,

22
00:00:42.027 --> 00:00:45.627
is you had these powerhouses of talent.

23
00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:46.998
Oh, unbelievable.

24
00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:48.945
Robert Carlock, who did 30 Rock,

25
00:00:48.945 --> 00:00:52.852
Kimmy Schmidt with Tina, Charlie Kaufman, of course,

26
00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.414
Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind,

27
00:00:54.414 --> 00:00:57.281
and many genius people, Louis C.K., incredible people

28
00:00:57.281 --> 00:01:00.203
working on it and just evaporated like a snowball

29
00:01:00.203 --> 00:01:03.186
coming in from low, in low orbit.

30
00:01:03.186 --> 00:01:04.155
It did not last.

31
00:01:04.155 --> 00:01:07.654
This was a primetime show, and this was back when--

32
00:01:07.654 --> 00:01:08.487
Primetime was primetime.

33
00:01:08.487 --> 00:01:11.402
Primetime gave you, they gave you a massive budget.

34
00:01:11.402 --> 00:01:13.294
This was a big network, massive budget.

35
00:01:13.294 --> 00:01:14.281
Oh, unbelievable!

36
00:01:14.281 --> 00:01:17.002
I had already worked for a small cable show called Exit 57,

37
00:01:17.002 --> 00:01:18.893
and we were like, you wrote a sketch and go like,

38
00:01:18.893 --> 00:01:20.025
"Oh, there's a jackhammer."

39
00:01:20.025 --> 00:01:21.521
And our producers would go, "Do you have a jackhammer?

40
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:22.354
"Because I don't have a jackhammer."

41
00:01:22.354 --> 00:01:23.881
(audience laughs)

42
00:01:23.881 --> 00:01:25.322
So I go over there.

43
00:01:25.322 --> 00:01:26.243
I'm living in Chicago.

44
00:01:26.243 --> 00:01:27.083
I get the job in New York.

45
00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:29.355
I fly to New York a month before my wife and our child

46
00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.635
came out there, so I'm living by myself in the city.

47
00:01:31.635 --> 00:01:33.865
And Robert Smigel, again, the executive producer goes,

48
00:01:33.865 --> 00:01:35.483
"Hey, you should pack a bag.

49
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:37.522
"We gotta go to Florida to shoot the second half

50
00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:40.043
"of a three-minute sketch that we shot in New York.

51
00:01:40.043 --> 00:01:41.915
"We're just gonna shoot the second half in Florida

52
00:01:41.915 --> 00:01:43.585
"because we need some warm weather for it."

53
00:01:43.585 --> 00:01:45.254
So I go, "Oh, okay."

54
00:01:45.254 --> 00:01:47.162
So I go down to Florida--

55
00:01:47.162 --> 00:01:49.075
For the second half of a three-minute sketch.

56
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.435
The second half of a three-minute sketch.

57
00:01:50.435 --> 00:01:52.065
That's the old network money.

58
00:01:52.065 --> 00:01:53.814
It just doesn't exist anymore.

59
00:01:53.814 --> 00:01:54.881
Only HBO has that.

60
00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.774
(audience laughs)

61
00:01:56.774 --> 00:01:59.385
So I go down to Florida, and I haven't talked to my wife.

62
00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.075
And there were no cellphones, only payphones.

63
00:02:01.075 --> 00:02:03.962
This is '95 or '96, something like that.

64
00:02:03.962 --> 00:02:06.534
So what we're doing is we're doing this thing

65
00:02:06.534 --> 00:02:08.883
where we're doing "animal experiments".

66
00:02:08.883 --> 00:02:12.955
I'm one of the Rockefellers in the Rockefeller Institute

67
00:02:12.955 --> 00:02:15.955
for Animal Studies or something, and we're just doing things

68
00:02:15.955 --> 00:02:18.635
like gluing things to mice and stuff like that.

69
00:02:18.635 --> 00:02:19.468
Not really.

70
00:02:19.468 --> 00:02:20.301
Don't hate me.

71
00:02:20.301 --> 00:02:22.345
This was the sketch premise.

72
00:02:22.345 --> 00:02:23.178
The sketch.

73
00:02:23.178 --> 00:02:24.443
And this sketch premise was we wanted to see

74
00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:26.974
what would happen if you threw a horse out of an airplane,

75
00:02:26.974 --> 00:02:28.281
(audience laughs)

76
00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.694
like what happens to its heart rate.

77
00:02:29.694 --> 00:02:31.921
It has a parachute and everything.

78
00:02:31.921 --> 00:02:35.323
So they build like, I shit thee not,

79
00:02:35.323 --> 00:02:39.921
like a $15,000 horse prop, articulated limbs, full size,

80
00:02:39.921 --> 00:02:42.995
drive it overnight all the way down to Florida.

81
00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:44.585
We're down there waiting for it to come.

82
00:02:44.585 --> 00:02:49.235
They put it in an old US Army constellation, jump a plane.

83
00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:53.254
They fly it over this field in Florida just for a test drop,

84
00:02:53.254 --> 00:02:54.654
just to see what it would look like

85
00:02:54.654 --> 00:02:55.694
before we rolled the cameras.

86
00:02:55.694 --> 00:02:56.722
No cameras rolling.

87
00:02:56.722 --> 00:02:57.745
Nobody's doing anything.

88
00:02:57.745 --> 00:02:58.963
And I decided, oh, I'll call my wife.

89
00:02:58.963 --> 00:03:02.155
So I call my wife on the phone, "Hey, hey Evie, hi.

90
00:03:02.155 --> 00:03:03.163
"Yes, listen."

91
00:03:03.163 --> 00:03:05.105
And I said, "Listen, I'm down in Florida."

92
00:03:05.105 --> 00:03:06.875
And she goes, "What are you doing in Florida?"

93
00:03:06.875 --> 00:03:09.275
I said, "Well, we have to throw the horse

94
00:03:09.275 --> 00:03:10.562
"out of the plane down here."

95
00:03:10.562 --> 00:03:12.014
(audience laughs)

96
00:03:12.014 --> 00:03:13.454
And she goes, "What horse out of what plane?"

97
00:03:13.454 --> 00:03:15.625
And I say, "Hold on, it's coming over right now."

98
00:03:15.625 --> 00:03:16.665
(audience laughs)

99
00:03:16.665 --> 00:03:19.115
And at that moment, the horse in the plane came over,

100
00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:23.315
and I just start shrieking because they pushed the horse

101
00:03:23.315 --> 00:03:26.574
out of the plane and becomes transcendently clear

102
00:03:26.574 --> 00:03:29.094
within seconds that the parachute is not gonna open up.

103
00:03:29.094 --> 00:03:31.155
(audience laughs)

104
00:03:31.155 --> 00:03:35.561
And I'm just on the phone going (shrieks).

105
00:03:35.561 --> 00:03:37.294
My wife thinks that I'm being killed,

106
00:03:37.294 --> 00:03:39.374
I'm being murdered because I'm just shrieking.

107
00:03:39.374 --> 00:03:41.955
And the horse plummets at terminal velocity

108
00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:44.494
and explodes when it hits the field.

109
00:03:44.494 --> 00:03:46.654
(Conan laughs)

110
00:03:46.654 --> 00:03:48.532
So we rebuilt it.

111
00:03:48.532 --> 00:03:50.242
We stayed a couple extra days.

112
00:03:50.242 --> 00:03:53.315
And I said to myself at that moment,

113
00:03:53.315 --> 00:03:56.715
I have to get back to that network money someday.

114
00:03:56.715 --> 00:03:58.355
(audience laughs)

115
00:03:58.355 --> 00:04:00.355
I tell stories now, we all tell stories

116
00:04:00.355 --> 00:04:02.105
about the early '90s and network TV.

117
00:04:02.105 --> 00:04:03.475
You remember this, Andy.

118
00:04:03.475 --> 00:04:06.675
Our writers, Louis C.K. was a writer on the show,

119
00:04:06.675 --> 00:04:08.921
it was his first job on TV, and Dino Stamatopoulos,

120
00:04:08.921 --> 00:04:11.214
all these great people were working on the show.

121
00:04:11.214 --> 00:04:13.201
And every night, they would pick

122
00:04:13.201 --> 00:04:15.715
the most expensive restaurant in New York,

123
00:04:15.715 --> 00:04:19.202
order food on it, and charge it to NBC.

124
00:04:19.202 --> 00:04:21.585
And I'd come in and they would be cracking lobsters,

125
00:04:21.585 --> 00:04:25.054
wearing giant bibs, and laughing. (laughs)

126
00:04:25.054 --> 00:04:26.763
(audience laughs)

127
00:04:26.763 --> 00:04:28.222
We went to a Kobe steakhouse in Florida

128
00:04:28.222 --> 00:04:30.353
on the same trip where, again, we're not shooting anything

129
00:04:30.353 --> 00:04:31.902
but the horse exploded.

130
00:04:31.902 --> 00:04:33.483
And we went to a Kobe steakhouse

131
00:04:33.483 --> 00:04:34.713
and we said, "We'll have the surf and turf."

132
00:04:34.713 --> 00:04:35.854
And they said, "We don't have that."

133
00:04:35.854 --> 00:04:37.433
And I said, "Bring us a full lobster dinner.

134
00:04:37.433 --> 00:04:39.212
"Bring us a whole Kobe steak dinner,

135
00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:40.305
"everybody at the table."

136
00:04:40.305 --> 00:04:41.764
I think that's why we were canceled.

137
00:04:41.764 --> 00:04:42.597
Yeah, yeah, exactly. (laughs)

138
00:04:42.597 --> 00:04:45.155
(audience laughs)

139
00:04:45.155 --> 00:04:47.395
We went through everything.

140
00:04:47.395 --> 00:04:49.895
The entire budget was gone.

